#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# transpose

#% text
# `transpose(m)` gives the matrix `m` with rows and columns exchanged.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `m` is a rectangular list of lists: every row has the same length. Row `i` of the result is column `i` of `m`.

#% item
# The entries may be anything: numbers, symbols, strings or lists. For a list of matrices only the first two levels are exchanged.

#% item
# Complex entries are not conjugated; use `conjugate` as well for the conjugate transpose.

#% item
# Rows of different lengths give `failure(shape_mismatch, transpose)`; a flat list gives a failure too.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
transpose([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])
#% output
#= [[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]

#% cell
transpose([[a, b], [c, d]])
#% output
#= [[a, c], [b, d]]

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# A row becomes a column and back:

#% cell
transpose([[1, 2, 3]])
#% output
#= [[1], [2], [3]]

#% cell
transpose([[1], [2], [3]])
#% output
#= [[1, 2, 3]]

#% text
# Entries of any kind:

#% cell
transpose([["a", "b"], ["c", "d"]])
#% output
#= [["a", "c"], ["b", "d"]]

#% text
# Only the first two levels are exchanged:

#% cell
transpose([[[1, 2], [3, 4]], [[5, 6], [7, 8]]])
#% output
#= [[[1, 2], [5, 6]], [[3, 4], [7, 8]]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The columns of a table of data, and their sums:

#% cell
data = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]; transpose(data)
#% output
#= [[1, 3, 5], [2, 4, 6]]

#% cell
map(total, transpose(data))
#% output
#= [9, 12]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# Transposing twice gives the matrix back:

#% cell
m = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]; transpose(transpose(m)) == m
#% output
#= true

#% text
# The transpose of a product is the product of the transposes in reverse order:

#% cell
p = [[0, 1], [1, 0]]; transpose(dot(m, p)) == dot(transpose(p), transpose(m))
#% output
#= true

#% text
# A symmetric matrix is its own transpose:

#% cell
s = [[1, 2], [2, 5]]; transpose(s) == s
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# A flat list of numbers is a vector, its own transpose:

#% cell
transpose([1, 2, 3])
#% output
#= [1, 2, 3]

#% text
# Rows must have the same length:

#% cell
transpose([[1, 2], [3]])
#% output
#! failure(shape_mismatch, transpose)

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `dot`, `conjugate`, `columns`, `rows`
